(window.__CLAIMS_ADD__||function(){})([{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"May 2026 regular at $4.479: crude 51.9%, refining 21.7%, distribution and marketing 14.8%, taxes 11.5% (read from EIA's data table, not the graphic)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-1-1"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"May 2026 diesel at $5.60: crude 41.5%, refining 25.3%, distribution and marketing 22.5%, taxes 10.7%","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-1-2"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The May 2026 split is \"the current one\" → it is EIA's latest published month, five months old, and the split moves 5–8 points month to month; say \"EIA's latest breakdown, May 2026\"","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-1-3"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"2022: crude 57% of the retail price, refinery margin 18%","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-1-4"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"2016–2025 average about 52% crude at $2.89; 2025 about 51% at $3.10 (refining 15.0/14.3, distribution 15.9/17.8, taxes 17.4/16.6)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-1-5"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"May 2026's refining share (21.7%) is high against the 2016–2025 mean (15.0%); \"high\", not \"unprecedented\" (Jan-26 19.7, Mar-26 21.3)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-1-6"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Crude oil is the largest component of the price","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-1-7"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Taxes are fixed cents, so their share falls as the price rises: 18.4% in January 2026 at $2.809, 11.5% in May at $4.479","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-1-8"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Federal gasoline tax 18.4 cents (18.3 excise + 0.1 storage-tank fee), unchanged since October 1993","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-2-1"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The stray \"1997\" date: in 1997 Congress redirected 4.3 cents to the Highway Trust Fund; the rate did not change","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-2-2"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"State tax averages 33.3 cents (1 January 2026), California 70.9, Alaska 9.0 → EIA's 1 July 2026 table: 33.8 cents state average, 52.2 with federal; California 73.6 (92.0 with federal); Alaska 9.0","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-2-3"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The three circulating averages (33.5, 33.3, 33.55) are EIA's own revisions, all superseded by the July table","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-2-4"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"26 states changed their gasoline taxes during 2025: 19 up, 7 down","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-2-5"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"California drivers pay about 90 cents in taxes; environmental compliance added up to 54 cents (March 2025)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-2-6"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Congress sets the federal rate in statute; no federal suspension has ever been enacted","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-2-7"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Each sustained $1 a barrel puts about 2.4 cents a gallon of pressure on gasoline (42 gallons a barrel); EIA, 2019","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-3-1"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"About half of a crude move reaches the pump in two weeks, about 80% in four; faster on the way up (EIA rule of thumb, 2019)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-3-2"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"St. Louis Fed: a $10 rise in a barrel goes with about 25 cents a gallon","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-3-3"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The St. Louis Fed \"calls the evidence inconclusive\" → its own finding is a small asymmetry, larger locally; \"inconclusive\" is its footnote on the wider literature","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-3-4"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"EIA's \"80% in four weeks\" and the Dallas Fed's \"about 50 percent after 20 working days\" are two different units (cents for cents vs percent for percent), not a conflict; air one and say which","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-3-5"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Borenstein, Cameron and Gilbert (1992 working paper, 1986–92 data): increases reach the pump within about 4 weeks, decreases over about 8","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-3-6"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"An FTC staff economist (2010): retail prices rise more than four times as fast as they fall, relative to wholesale rack prices (not crude)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-3-7"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Bachmeier–Griffin (no asymmetry in daily wholesale), Faber (38% of stations), Cook–Fosten (mixed replications)","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-3-8"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Kilian: in the long run 59% of US real gasoline-price variation comes from oil-market-specific demand shocks, 33% from global demand, 8% from refining; US gasoline demand and oil supply shocks under 1%","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-3-9"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Military action involving Iran began on 28 February 2026 (EIA's wording; no aggressor named)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-4-1"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The Strait of Hormuz carries nearly 20% of world oil supply and was \"effectively closed\", not physically blocked","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-4-2"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Brent about $71 on 27 February","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-4-3"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"March average $103, $32 above February","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-4-4"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"\"Almost $128 on 2 April\" is not the peak: the 2026 daily high in EIA's series is $138.21 on 7 April; April averaged $117.29, the highest month","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-4-5"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"A ceasefire announced in early April; the strait stayed largely at a standstill, with a new US blockade of Iranian oil shipments","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-4-6"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Hormuz flows 21.6 → 4.9 million barrels a day, cited to the September STEO → it is the August STEO","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-4-7"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"An 18 June US–Iran memorandum of understanding; Brent fell to about $69 on 2 July, then rose to $105 on 23 July after renewed tanker attacks; August averaged $91 with a renewed US blockade and new sanctions","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-4-8"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"September averaged $114, with a $131 spot on 15 September after attacks on Saudi Arabia's East-West pipeline","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-4-9"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"EIA forecasts \"about $90 in the second half of 2026\" and $3.84 retail for 2026 → superseded on 6 October: Brent $105 in 4Q26, retail $3.91 for 2026, just under $3.60 for 2027","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-4-10"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"\"Brent about $105 in 4Q26 with about 4.5 million b/d of Middle East shut-ins\", cited to the April STEO → those are October STEO numbers (April had $88); shut-ins peaked at 10.9 million b/d in May and were 4.8 in September","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-4-11"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"EIA ties the smaller rise in the US crude benchmark partly to the reserve announcement (11 March) and a 60-day Jones Act waiver (17 March), with no figure","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-4-12"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"A drop in Hormuz flows is not the same as lost supply: production shut-ins were far smaller, because of bypass routes","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-4-13"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"\"$135 Dated Brent vs about $100 futures on 2 October\" → EIA's series is \"Europe Brent Spot Price FOB\" (physical cargoes; EIA does not call it Dated Brent): $135.51 on 2 October was a one-day spike ($125.51 next trading day); in September spot averaged $114.16 against a $102.02 front-month future; same-day futures for 2 October unresolved","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-4-14"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"March's +$32 a barrel implies about 77 cents a gallon of sustained pressure","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-4-15"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"February's $70.89 average to September's $114.16 is +$43 a barrel, about $1.04 a gallon of pressure","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-4-16"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Regular gasoline $4.354 (week of 5 October 2026) vs $3.124 a year earlier: +$1.23, +39.4%; 2026 weekly high $4.500 (11 May); all-time weekly high $5.006 (13 June 2022)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-4-17"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The reserve held 282,983 thousand barrels in the week ending 2 October 2026, its lowest since the week ending 15 October 1982 (280,802); the series peak was 726,617 (2010)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-4-18"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"That is about 40% of the 713.5 million barrels of authorized capacity; DOE estimates it can effectively hold about 680","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-4-19"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"On 2 October 2026 G7 leaders agreed a coordinated release through the IEA of 100 million barrels over four months, with a front-loaded diesel release in the first 20 days","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-5-1"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The 100 million barrels are \"on top of\" the IEA's 400 million → the statement ties them to implementing the March 2026 commitments; say \"to deliver the March pledge\", never \"a further 100 million\"","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-5-2"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The G7 also agreed to coordinate refinery maintenance and raise runs where feasible, and not to restrict energy exports among themselves","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-5-3"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The G7 deal was tied to a US threat to ban diesel exports","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-5-4"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"IEA members agreed on 11 March 2026 to make 400 million barrels available, the largest release in its history and its sixth; the IEA calls it a stop-gap and gives no price effect","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-5-5"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The US share is 172 million barrels","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-5-6"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"\"A 172-million-barrel US share against an observed draw of about 130\" (named as an inconsistency) → not a conflict: 172 is a plan run as exchanges and still in progress; about 133 million awarded, 132.5 million drawn from 20 March to 2 October, the last 40 million due in November and December","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-5-7"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The US release is run as exchanges: loans repaid with premium barrels, about 25%","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-5-8"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"About 325 of the 400 million barrels had reached the market by October","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-5-9"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Country shares beyond the US (Japan 80, Korea 22.5, Germany 19.5, UK 13.5)","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-5-10"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The reserve held about 415 million barrels at the end of February 2026","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-5-11"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"EIA's October outlook models the reserve at about 242.5 million barrels by the end of 2026","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-5-12"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"100 million barrels over about four months is about 0.8 million barrels a day, roughly a sixth of September's 4.8 million b/d of shut-in production","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-5-13"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Treasury (2022) estimated the US release cut gasoline by 13–31 cents, 17–42 cents with IEA partners, assuming full pass-through","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-6-1"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"\"The 33- and 38-cent point estimates came from a garbled table; use the ranges\" → the table is clean: 33 cents US-only and 38 cents with partners, from a second method","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-6-2"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"\"Newell and Priest 2017\" → Newell and Prest","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-6-3"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Treasury's model assumed demand elasticities of 0.08–0.20 and zero crude-supply response, and did not account for a 17-million-barrel rise in private stocks","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-6-4"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Treasury itself says pass-through to the pump is unclear","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-6-5"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"2022: 180 million US barrels over six months plus 60 million from IEA partners (Treasury, 26 July 2022)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-6-6"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The White House's \"up to about 40 cents\" is the top of the combined range","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-6-7"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Other analysts put 2022's effect at about 15 cents, and earlier releases at \"a few cents\"","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-6-8"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Newell and Prest: a 10-million-barrel release temporarily lowers spot prices about 2–3%; past releases about 15–20%","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-6-9"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Kilian and Zhou find releases \"statistically indistinguishable from zero\" → reserve shocks explain about 8% of oil-price swings and a single shock's effect is weak, but actual interventions \"have worked\", modestly","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-6-10"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"\"1990–91 and 2005 together cut the oil price by at most $2\" → about $2 a barrel each (about 5 cents a gallon at full pass-through)","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-6-11"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Libya 2011: about $10–12 a barrel, peaking near $13 (up to about 31 cents a gallon at full pass-through)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-6-12"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The authors call the modest results contrary to \"folk wisdom\"; the IEA says its actions played \"at least a partial role\"","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-6-13"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Private inventories partly offset a release","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-6-14"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Exchanges ultimately raise the oil price; emergency drawdowns lower it","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-6-15"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"\"2000\" as a past reserve release → it was a 30-million-barrel exchange; DOE counts four emergency releases (1991, 2005, 2011, 2022)","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-6-16"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Release sizes: 1991 17.3 million delivered (33.75 planned); 2005 20.8 million (11 sold, 9.8 loaned); 2011 30.6 million; 2022 180 million","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-6-17"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Oil can begin moving within 13 days of a release order","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-6-18"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Katrina (2005) was a gasoline shortage, not a crude shortage","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-6-19"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"After the November 2021 announcement Brent rose; the release was \"already priced in\"","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-6-20"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"November 2021: 32 of 50 million barrels were exchanges, and \"the rally faded, WTI back below $80 within weeks\" → ING, writing the next day, said WTI was already below $80 after weeks of talk and called it \"relatively successful\"","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-6-21"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"A measured, after-the-fact estimate of the 2022 release's effect","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-6-22"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"2022's emergency sales averaged about $95; DOE bought 59 million barrels back at under $76","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-6-23"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"200 million barrels secured at $74.75 on average, 140 million of them by cancelling mandated sales","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-6-24"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Drawdown capability is 61% of design (2.7 vs 4.415 million b/d, December 2025)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-6-25"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"More than a quarter of the crude was unavailable; future capability \"highly uncertain\" (GAO, May 2026)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-6-26"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"92.6 million barrels of mandated sales remain through 2031; no target size has been set","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-6-27"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"2022 needed about 20% of the design drawdown rate and 31% of the inventory","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-6-28"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"As a net exporter the US has no IEA stockholding obligation","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-6-29"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Refill cost at 2026 prices, and days of cover at 283 million barrels","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-6-30"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"2022 state holidays passed through 83% (Maryland), 82% (Florida), 62% (Georgia), 56% (Connecticut), about zero (New York)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-7-1"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Maryland's fell almost immediately: 29 cents of 36","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-7-2"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"New York's prices rose about 10 cents in the weeks before its holiday","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-7-3"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Illinois and Indiana (2000): prices fell 3% and rose 4%; full pass-through rejected; 54–78%","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-7-4"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Earlier work: about 50% of federal excise tax reaches consumers, 75% of state sales taxes, nearly all of state excise","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-7-5"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"2026: Georgia suspended its 33.3-cent tax from 20 March to 2 June, and 64% reached the pump; Indiana 40% at first, 76% combined (Dallas Fed working paper, August 2026, preliminary)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-7-6"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Georgia's prices took 63.9% of the cut but only 50.3% of the reinstatement, ending about 14% of the tax below where they started","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-7-7"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"A federal holiday at measured pass-through: about 12 cents on gasoline, 20 on diesel, about $8 billion over four months, about 16% of the shock's burden (a model)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-7-8"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"A state holiday offset the same share (28%) of the per-gallon burden across income groups","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-7-9"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The federal ceiling (18.4 cents) is about 15% of the year's $1.23 rise; CRS makes the same point for spring 2026","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-7-10"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"In 2026 the President and several Members of Congress proposed suspending the federal tax","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-7-11"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Maryland's 30-day holiday cost $93.6 million, $91.6 million from its transportation fund, backfilled with $100 million of general funds","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-7-12"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"CRFB's $20 billion 2022 federal holiday covered \"both gasoline and diesel\" → its text describes the gasoline tax and storage-tank fee; $20 billion and the trust-fund year are right; advocacy, not a CBO score","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-7-13"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"A May–September 2026 federal suspension would cost about $17 billion (Bipartisan Policy Center, cited by CRS); no CBO or JCT score was found","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-7-14"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Georgia's 2022 suspension cost about $950 million (the governor's estimate, via news)","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-7-15"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Connecticut's April–June 2022 suspension cost about $90 million (business-group page)","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-7-16"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Florida's October 2022 holiday cost about $200 million","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-7-17"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Economists' panel, March 2026: 51.3% agree a temporary federal suspension would deliver substantially lower prices, 33.3% disagree","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-7-18"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"E15 sold 3–10 cents below regular E10 in most markets, at under 2% of stations (2019): a blend discount, not a waiver effect","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-8-1"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"\"The 2026 waiver letters were not located\" → a national waiver of 25 March 2026 (E15, a volatility allowance, federal low-volatility rules and state boutique fuels waived), renewed in 20-day steps; from 1 September all federal volatility standards waived","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-8-2"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The 2026 letter gives a supply rationale and no price figure; no agency has measured the pump effect of a waiver","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-8-3"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The 2026 letter cites the National Energy Emergency declared on 20 January 2025","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-8-4"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Summer low-volatility rules exist to limit ozone pollution","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-8-5"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Full Jones Act repeal: about $0.63 a barrel lower East Coast gasoline in 2018–19 (about 1.5 cents a gallon); Gulf Coast up about $0.30 (a working paper, not peer reviewed)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-8-6"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The one study of past waivers found no clear evidence they lowered prices","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-8-7"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Past waivers: 18 days after Katrina, Sandy, 2017, and 2011 to move reserve oil; granted for national defense only","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-8-8"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"A waiver is an executive act (Defense or Homeland Security); repeal is Congress's","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-8-9"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Analysts call the 2026 waiver's effect \"pennies\" (trade press)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-8-10"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"\"JPMorgan's 2022 estimate of about 10 cents\" from the same piece → not in that source","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-8-11"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Economists' panel, March 2026, on a temporary price cap: 0% agree, 89.7% disagree, \"18 disagree + 17 strongly disagree\" → 17 disagree + 18 strongly disagree, 4 uncertain, of 39","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-9-1"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"1979: US crude supply fell 0.6–0.7 million b/d in the first four months against 1978 (GAO)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-9-2"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"\"DOE allocation rules made firms cut gasoline by more than their lost Iranian imports\" → DOE rules were one of several reasons GAO lists, beside the companies' own crude-allocation practices","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-9-3"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Federal oil price and allocation controls were ended by executive order on 28 January 1981","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-9-4"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"EIA's 2012 market reports list tighter Iran sanctions among factors without attributing a price change","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-9-5"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"2018: EIA assumed about 1 million b/d lost from Iran","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-9-6"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Brent hit $86 on 3 October 2018, then eased; sanctions waivers helped","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-9-7"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Venezuela 2019: EIA expected no significant drop in US refinery runs","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-9-8"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The Urals discount was due to \"the EU embargo\" and the Russian price cap's effect was negligible → the embargo \"led by the U.S. and the European Union\"; cap negligible","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-9-9"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"\"No modelled effect of a US crude export ban was found\" → EIA (2015) modelled the ban's repeal: US gasoline unchanged or slightly lower, because pump prices follow Brent; product-export bans remain unmodelled","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-9-10"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Requests to OPEC by presidents of both parties (2018, 2022) show no documented production response; in 2022 OPEC+ cut its quota by 2 million b/d","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-9-11"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"2026 OPEC+ quota rises largely existed on paper","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-9-12"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"\"No EIA lease-to-production lag figure exists\" → EIA puts ANWR at about 10 years to first oil (2008 and 2018 analyses)","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-9-13"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"EIA's 2008 ANWR model: oil prices $0.41–$1.44 a barrel lower at peak, 17–19 years out; OPEC could neutralise it","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-9-14"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"About 12% of offshore and 5% of onshore leases (1987–96) ever produced","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-9-15"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Offshore production 2–3 years from sale (BOEM 2007); onshore years to a decade (industry)","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-9-16"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"California's unexplained surcharge averaged 41 cents a gallon in 2015–2024 ($59 billion), peaking at $1.36 (fall 2022)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-9-17"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The state regulator says it \"may be associated with\" market power; four companies control about 90% of in-state refining","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-9-18"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"March 2012: 36 of 39 economists on the panel agreed a decade of gas-price changes was predominantly market-driven, not federal policy; none disagreed (3 uncertain, 2 no answer)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-10-1"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The 2012 statement is one retrospective question about what predominantly drove prices; it does not ask whether a president can move prices in the short run","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-10-2"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"\"Economists agree a president can't move gas prices\" → they agree market forces mostly drive the price; a 2026 survey has a bit over half of energy experts saying a president has no or very little influence","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-10-3"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Brewer and Oliver (Energy Economics, 2026): 57.1% of energy experts say no or very little influence; average prices differed significantly across presidents 2003–2022 after controls, with no causal claim","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-10-4"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"March 2026 panel: 20.5% agree the IEA release would deliver substantially lower US pump prices over six months; 46.2% disagree","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-10-5"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"A 2022 panel on gas prices or the reserve","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-10-6"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The biggest levers against the year's $1.23: Treasury's 13–42 cents is about 11–34%; the federal tax ceiling about 15%","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-10-7"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Presidential approval tends to fall when real gas prices rise (monthly data 1973–2023); the authors also say presidents have limited control over energy prices","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-11-1"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"\"A 0.60% decrease in approval per 10 cents\" → 0.6 percentage points, in one model, January 1976–July 2007, in the author's own words in an interview (the chapter was not opened; the figure stays off air)","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-11-2"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Kim and Yang: voters with longer commutes are more likely to hold the president accountable for gas-price rises","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-11-3"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Whether people say a president can do \"a lot\" about gas prices flips with the party in the White House: Democrats 71% (2006) → 42% (2012); Republicans 54% → 69%","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-11-4"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"2022: 65% said the president has at least some control, 25% not; the party of the sitting president is what matters","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-11-5"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"March 2026: a lot of control 33%, a little 37%, none 14%, not sure 16%","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-11-6"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"\"Every president is blamed for rises and credited for falls\" → blame on the way up, little or no credit on the way down","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-11-7"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Further approval papers (Fox 2009, Coggin 2024, Burkhart 2025, Brewer and Oliver full text)","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-11-8"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The \"October 2026\" KFF poll ties gas with health costs → fielded 16–21 September, released 6 October; 62% of adults worried about gas (27% very), 62% about health care; gas worry eased from April's 64%","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-11-9"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"April 2026: gas worry at 64%, tied with health care; \"very worried\" up from 17% to 29% since January, when gas ranked last","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-11-10"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Gas worry was higher in 2022: 71% (March), 76% (July)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-11-11"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"91% of registered voters say the conflict is making oil and gas prices higher, including 97% of Democrats and 84% of Republicans (30 Sept–2 Oct 2026)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-11-12"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"\"Very concerned\" about gas prices rose from 34% (January) to 56% (July 2026), the largest rise of any economic item (half-sample wording)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-11-13"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"March 2026: 69% concerned about higher gas prices from the war, Democrats 79%, Republicans 59%","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-11-14"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"June 2026: two-thirds report fuel-cost hardship (17% severe, below 2022's 22%); under $50,000 77% (28% severe), $100,000+ 59% (8% severe); 57% drive less","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-11-15"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"A 2026 poll split on blaming the President by party (97% vs 22%)","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-11-16"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Weekly peaks: 2008 $4.114 (7 July), 2022 $5.006 (13 June), 2026 so far $4.500 (11 May); 2026 low $2.796 (5 January)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-12-1"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"In October 2026 dollars: June 2008 $6.22, June 2022 $5.57, May 2026 $4.47 (2026's high month), September 2026 $4.36; so 2026's worst month is about a fifth below 2022's and over a quarter below 2008's (September–October CPI are EIA estimates until BLS publishes)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-12-2"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"25 April 2006, the President: \"What people are seeing at their gasoline pumps reflects the global economy in which we live.\" The price rose to $3.038 (7 Aug), then fell to $2.200 (6 Nov)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-12-3"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"14 July 2008, the President lifted the executive ban on offshore drilling; the price fell from $4.113 to $1.613 by 29 December, in the financial crash, before any new lease could produce","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-12-4"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"23 February 2012, the President: \"no silver bullets short term\"; \"The oil market is global\". The price rose to $3.941 (2 April), then fell to $3.356 (2 July)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-12-5"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"22 June 2022, the President called for a 90-day federal holiday; the price was $4.962, already off its peak, and fell to $3.091 by 26 December","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-12-6"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"May 2026, the President on suspending the federal tax (\"It's very temporary\"); the price eased from $4.490 to $3.777 (6 July) during the ceasefire, then rose to $4.478 (21 September)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-12-7"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Presidents of different parties called for the same federal holiday (2022, 2026); Congress enacted neither","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-12-8"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"A typical vehicle uses about 491 gallons a year (FHWA 2023) → FHWA's newer 2024 table: 460 gallons, 10,787 miles, 23.4 mpg","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-13-1"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Each 10 cents costs about $49 a year and the year's $1.23 about $604 → at 460 gallons: 10 cents ≈ $46 a year, $1 ≈ $460, $1.23 ≈ $566 (about $47 a month) per car; a full federal holiday ≈ $85 a year","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-13-2"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Per driver, 13,476 miles a year","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-13-3"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The US burned about 376 million gallons of gasoline a day in 2023, so each 10 cents is about $13.7 billion a year nationally","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-13-4"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"2022 spending on \"gasoline, other fuels, and motor oil\": 4.8%, 5.0%, 5.1%, 4.8%, 3.3% of spending by income fifth; $1,553 bottom vs $4,601 top","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-13-5"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"As a share of pre-tax income, about 10.9% for the bottom fifth vs 1.9% for the top, with the caveat that reported low incomes understate resources (the bottom fifth's after-tax income exceeds its pre-tax mean)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-13-6"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"New York Fed, May 2026: households under $40,000 raised gas spending 12% and cut real gas use 7%; over $125,000, +19% and −1%","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-13-7"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Households average about 1.9 vehicles (BLS 2022)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-13-8"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Poterba (spending-based burden less regressive); Wadud et al. (rural −0.091 vs urban −0.577 elasticities)","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-13-9"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"An example 15-gallon fill-up: $65.31 now vs $46.86 a year ago, $18.45 more","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-13-10"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Georgia paused its fuel tax again from 29 September to 29 October 2026, with Ohio and Indiana acting too","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-14-1"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"A Penn study: Maryland 72%, Georgia 58–65%","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-14-2"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Larger pass-through lowered households' inflation expectations","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-14-3"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"283 million barrels is about two weeks of US petroleum use","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-14-4"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Brent is up about $58 a barrel on a year ago, about $1.39 a gallon","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-14-5"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Smoother driving saves 10–40% of fuel; each 5 mph over 50 costs 7% or more","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-14-6"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Station prices within one city differed by $1.13 on average (2016)","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-14-7"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"OPEC+ held November output on 4 October 2026","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-14-8"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The 2026 exchanges are \"saving taxpayers more than $3 billion\"","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-14-9"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"E15 waivers save 25–47 cents (industry and agency figures)","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-14-10"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"\"US oil is about $90 a barrel\" → US crude (WTI, Cushing spot) averaged $97.31 in September 2026 and ran $96.13–99.77 a day from 29 Sept to 6 Oct; \"$90\" fits March ($91.38)","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-15-1"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The world benchmark is much higher than US crude: Brent spot $114.16 in September vs WTI $97.31; $125.44 vs $96.24 on 6 Oct; the 2025 monthly gap was $2.28–4.59. Futures show about $6 (Brent $102.02 vs WTI $95.69, Sept). Air one, and say which","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-15-2"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"US crude rose less than the world price because of US inventories and policy interventions","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-15-3"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"At this week's US crude price the barrel is about $2.32 of the $4.354 gallon, about 53%","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-15-4"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Refining (Gulf Coast wholesale gasoline minus that crude) is about $1.31 a gallon, about 30%","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-15-5"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Taxes are about 52 cents, 12% of this week's gallon","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-15-6"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The station and distribution slice is thin this year (about 20 cents, about 5%)","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-15-7"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The refiner's gasoline margin over US crude is about double normal: 2015–2025 annual averages $0.19 (2020) to $0.69 (2022), 2025 $0.46; 2026 Jan–Sep $0.97; September $1.415 a gallon ($59 a barrel)","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-15-8"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The diesel margin is higher still: $2.569 a gallon over WTI in September; retail diesel's weekly record $6.529 on 21 Sept 2026 (2022 peak $5.810)","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-15-9"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"EIA: rising crack spreads drove part of September's rise and will stay above seasonal averages into early 2027","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-15-10"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"\"Measured against the world crude price, the US gasoline margin looks ordinary\" → part of the wide margin is US refiners buying cheaper US crude and selling fuel at a world price (about $0.40 a gallon in September, about $0.56 this week), but even over Brent the margin is about double 2025's: $0.748 this week and $1.014 in September vs a 2025 average of about $0.37 (monthly range $0.20–0.50). A margin is not a profit; no finding of wrongdoing is in this record","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-15-11"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The US has been a net petroleum exporter since 2020","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-15-12"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"2026 net petroleum exports are the largest on record (May 2026, about 5.9 million b/d)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-15-13"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"US crude production is about 13.8–14.0 million b/d in 2026","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-15-14"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The US still imports more crude than it exports (Jan–Jul 2026: 6.28 vs 4.56 million b/d); the net-exporter status comes from crude and fuels together","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-15-15"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Canada supplies most US crude imports: 63% in 2025, 61.8–68.0% a month in 2026","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-15-16"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Persian Gulf crude was about 7.9% of US crude imports in 2025 and about 1.9% by June 2026; July's cell is blank","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-15-17"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"\"The US doesn't depend on the Middle East\": true for the barrels, false for the price","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-15-18"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"US exports of crude and fuels rose about 13% in 2026 (Jan–Jul mean 12,152 vs 10,785 thousand b/d); crude exports hit a series high in May (5,728)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-15-19"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"US diesel exports about 1.5 million b/d in 2026, a record 1.6 in August","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-15-20"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Many US refineries are built for heavier crude than US wells produce, which is why they import (mostly from Canada) while the US exports light crude","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-15-21"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"US gasoline is priced off the world crude price (Brent), not US crude (WTI)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-15-22"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Gasoline is a world commodity","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-15-23"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"When US crude was last much cheaper than Brent (2011–2013), the pump followed Brent","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-15-24"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Regions differ: week of 5 Oct, Gulf Coast $3.911, Midwest $4.109, East Coast $4.147, West Coast $5.727, California $6.227; the coasts import some gasoline","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-15-25"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Lifting the crude export ban (2015) left US gasoline unchanged or slightly lower (re-quoted; 9.10)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-15-26"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Keeping crude at home cheapens US crude; the gain goes to refiners' margins while fuel follows the world price","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-15-27"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"2022: a bank note modelled a US product-export ban as raising fuel margins abroad and forcing US refiners to slow","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-15-28"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"September 2026: a diesel export limit was publicly considered; two House bills filed 17 Sept (H.R. 10423, a ban until January 2027; H.R. 10422, a $5-diesel trigger); not enacted as of 8 Oct","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-15-29"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Modelled effect of a 2026 diesel export ban: refiners cut runs about 12%, gasoline output falls up to 750,000 b/d, the US becomes a net gasoline importer","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-15-30"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"New England and the West Coast rely on imports by sea, so a ban would trap Gulf Coast supply","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-15-31"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"A government (EIA, CBO or DOE) model of a product-export ban's pump effect exists","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-15-32"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"In past months with US crude at $85–100 (since 2007) regular cost $2.80–3.98 and the pump ran $0.76–1.75 above the barrel; in September 2026, $4.355 and $2.04, the widest","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-15-33"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The same comparison in today's dollars","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-15-34"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Federal Register, 20 Sept–8 Oct 2026: no fuel-tax, dyed-diesel or EPA diesel-waiver document published","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-16-1"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The instrument is an executive order, \"Emergency Tax Relief on Diesel Fuel\", signed 5 October 2026; not yet in the Federal Register on 8 Oct","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-16-2"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The President announced it in Nebraska and signed it that night","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-16-3"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"\"The President removed the taxes on diesel\" → the order asks Treasury to decide whether it may defer (not cancel) the federal tax on diesel used on the road that was not taxed at the terminal (in practice, dyed diesel), for fuel used 5 Oct–31 Dec 2026; clear diesel still pays 24.4c","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-16-4"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Deferral is not forgiveness; cancelling the deferred tax is only something Treasury is told to \"explore\", including through legislation","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-16-5"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"\"Allowed dyed diesel on the highway\" → federal penalty relief only: the IRS is to announce it won't impose the dyed-fuel penalty for road use 5 Oct–31 Dec; no announcement on 8 Oct; state law and EPA rules untouched","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-16-6"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"\"Allow anyone to purchase tax-free red dye diesel for any reason\" (the release) → the signed order defers \"to the extent authorized by law\", conditional on Treasury's §7508A finding","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-16-7"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The legal hook (§7508A) lets Treasury postpone tax deadlines up to a year for a federally declared disaster, a significant fire, or a terroristic or military action; the order brings in the Secretary of War","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-16-8"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Window 5 Oct–31 Dec 2026; Treasury and IRS steps due within 5 days (about 10 Oct)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-16-9"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Diesel tax 24.4c; dyed diesel pays only the 0.1c LUST fee; the dyed-fuel penalty is the greater of $1,000 or $10 a gallon","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-16-10"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Off-road diesel is untaxed and dyed red so inspectors can spot it on the road","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-16-11"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The order did not waive EPA fuel rules; EPA's diesel waiver that week was for Texas only (higher-sulfur diesel, 5–24 Oct, at the state's request)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-16-12"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"EPA rules bar visible red dye in highway diesel unless EPA waives it","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-16-13"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The order tells FMCSA to keep enforcing, and tells USDA and the White House intergovernmental office to \"encourage\" states","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-16-14"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The White House text blames diesel's rise on the Russia-Ukraine war and a lack of refining capacity, adding a partisan line about state refinery closures","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-16-15"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"\"[The President] successfully negotiated with Europe to release 100 million barrels of refined diesel from its strategic reserves\" (the fact sheet) → the G7 text is a coordinated IEA release of 100 million barrels of oil stocks by G7 members and partners, diesel front-loaded","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-16-16"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"DOT waived its hours-of-service rules for fuel haulers","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-16-17"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"South Dakota's governor (6 Oct) told highway patrol not to enforce, but said he cannot waive state taxes or penalties set in statute","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-16-18"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"South Dakota's revenue department (8 Oct) narrowed it to farmers; others still owe state and municipal tax and may owe federal tax in January","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-16-19"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Montana's transportation department (7 Oct): the state ban still stands","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-16-20"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Wisconsin: the governor lacks power to suspend the statute; legislation needed","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-16-21"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Washington: dyed diesel still generally banned in licensed road vehicles","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-16-22"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"New York: state law still bars it","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-16-23"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Kentucky's governor and attorney general (7 Oct) suspended the state ban and penalties, capped at the statute's 30 days","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-16-24"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Iowa's governor (6 Oct) suspended state penalties, for farm loads only, through 2 Feb 2027","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-16-25"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"North Carolina (1 Oct, before the order): farm-use penalty relief only, the state's 41c excise still owed, in-state only","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-16-26"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Illinois's governor (6 Oct) told revenue and state police not to impose penalties through 31 Dec; retailers wary","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-16-27"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Before the order, 10 states (about a third of diesel sales) had eased dyed-diesel rules (23 Sept–2 Oct)","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-16-28"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"\"States and municipalities say the federal change doesn't change the local laws\" → right on the law (no state law fell because of the order), misleading as a picture: more states moved toward it, mostly for farm loads, than refused; no municipal statement was found","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-16-29"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"A trucker who crosses a state line can fall back under a ban","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-16-30"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Dye lingers in the tank, so users must flush before the relief ends","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-16-31"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"US on-highway diesel $6.199 on 5 Oct 2026 (from $6.529 on 21 Sept); $3.71 a year earlier","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-16-32"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The federal piece is worth at most 24.4c a gallon, about 3.9% of a $6.20 gallon, only on dyed diesel used on the road, and deferred","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-16-33"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The White House: about $60 on a 250-gallon fill; over $100 where states match","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-16-34"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"A 250-gallon fill costs about $1,575 and could fall about $150 with no federal or state tax; state diesel taxes average 35.5c","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-16-35"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"USDA: about $640 million in combined federal and state savings for farms (an estimate that assumes states join; no method published)","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-16-36"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The owner-operator truckers' group calls it \"minimal relief\"","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-16-37"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Transport diesel is about 14% of US petroleum use; diesel moves \"nearly all products people consume\"","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-16-38"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The order's effect on the price of goods","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-16-39"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The order adds no fuel; more dyed demand at harvest and the start of heating season can strain dyed supply","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-16-40"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Six 2026 federal fuel-tax holiday bills (H.R. 3768, 7919, 8572, 8772; S. 4032, 4485) all sit at referral to committee, none voted on","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-16-41"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"September 2026: a House push for a vote on a three-month fuel-tax suspension was dropped before the pre-election recess","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-16-42"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The diesel order's relief was not in force on 8 Oct: no Treasury §7508A determination and no IRS announcement posted","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-16-43"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"2022: after a president's call for a holiday (12.6), the bills (S. 3609, H.R. 6787) never got past referral","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-16-44"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Only Congress can suspend or cut the federal fuel tax; a President alone can postpone when it is paid (a qualifying disaster or military action, up to a year), choose not to penalise dye misuse, and ask states and Congress","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-16-45"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"CNN calls the order \"a pre-election tax holiday\"","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-16-46"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Refiners' margins per barrel in spring 2026 (Q2) were about double spring 2025: Marathon $36.33 vs $17.58, Valero $23.62 vs $12.35, Phillips 66 $24.08 vs $11.25, PBF $23.40 vs $8.38 (HF Sinclair +57%); ratios 1.57x–2.79x","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-17-1"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Profit per barrel grew faster than the margin because running costs barely moved: Valero operating income per barrel about 3.5x, Marathon's segment EBITDA per barrel about 3.7x","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-17-2"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The four largest independents (Marathon, Valero, Phillips 66, PBF) made about $13.6 billion of net income in Q2 2026, vs about $2.8bn in Q2 2025 and about $14.9bn in Q2 2022","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-17-3"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Spring 2026 did not beat spring 2022 per barrel: margins 77–97% of Q2 2022","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-17-4"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Q1 2026 (January–March) was ordinary; the jump came in spring","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-17-5"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"2025 was a weak year for these refiners, so \"double 2025\" starts from a low base","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-17-6"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Q3 2026 (July–September, when the September crack peaked) is not yet reported","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-17-7"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"What a refining margin is","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-17-8"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Running the plant, shipping the fuel, maintenance shutdowns and equipment wear come out of the margin before profit, then interest and tax","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-17-9"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The renewable-fuel credit bill (RINs) more than doubled in 2026, by Valero's market reference","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-17-10"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Per gallon, Valero kept about 39 cents of operating profit in spring 2026, vs about 11 cents in spring 2025 and 54 cents in spring 2022","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-17-11"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The companies say the war cut world fuel supply (refineries and export terminals damaged, shipping disrupted)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-17-12"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Fuel inventories are low, especially diesel (the companies and EIA); East Coast diesel inventories 32% below normal in September","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-17-13"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Crack spreads roughly doubled (the companies and EIA)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-17-14"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"US refining capacity shrank: two closures in 2025 (about 400,000 b/d) and Benicia in early 2026; the effect is largest on the West Coast","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-17-15"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Exports pulled US fuel abroad (exports up; the link to margins is approximate)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-17-16"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"US refiners buy cheaper US crude and sell at the world price, but by Valero's own futures reference the discount was only about $4 a barrel in Q2 2026","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-17-17"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Wrongdoing (collusion, withheld supply) explains the margin","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-17-18"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"DOJ and the FTC (3 July 2026) told state attorneys general they are \"closely monitoring\" petroleum markets and urged states to investigate: a call to look, no finding, no company named","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-17-19"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Two US senators wrote (11 June 2026) to seven oil producers asking why pump prices stayed high: questions, not findings; none went to the refiners in 17.1","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-17-20"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"California's petroleum watchdog (DPMO) opened a look at about 20 of the highest-priced gas stations (March 2026); no result published","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-17-21"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"California's watchdog found California's rise in line with the rest of the US, and flagged a brand-name premium and a thin diesel price-reporting market, not refiner collusion","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-17-22"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"California's refiner margin cap is not in force: the Energy Commission resolved (29 Aug 2025) to take no action on it for at least five years","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-17-23"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"California refiners' self-reported gasoline margin averaged about 97 c/gal Jan–Jul 2026, up from about 75 c in 2025, below 2023's about $1.09","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-17-24"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"The FTC opened a 2026 investigation of refiners' pricing","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-17-25"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"A state attorney general opened a 2026 investigation of refiners","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-17-26"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Lucas's \"WTI is 91.06 right now\" (8 Oct) is the front-month futures price, not EIA's spot","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-17-27"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"EIA's latest spot WTI (Cushing) is $96.24 (6 Oct 2026); September average $97.31","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-17-28"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"\"EIA's front-month futures series (RCLC1) gives today's futures price\" → EIA stopped publishing daily futures after 5 April 2024","v":"Corrected before air","r":""},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"\"$91 today vs ~$97 in September\" is partly a real fall and partly two different prices; since 28 Sep EIA spot runs about $6.70–6.90 above front-month futures, unexplained in the record","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-17-30"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"14 Oct: the IRS announced it will not impose any penalty related to the excise tax when dyed diesel is sold for use or used on highways, retroactive to 1 Oct and through 31 Dec 2026; the §7508A postponement of the tax itself awaits further guidance; federal only, no effect on state taxes","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-17-33"},{"s":"ep38-can-a-president-lower-gas","n":38,"c":"Treasury or the IRS posted the dyed-diesel deferral or penalty relief on 8 Oct","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-17-32"}]);